#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# rank

#% text
# `rank(m)` gives the rank of the matrix `m`, the number of linearly independent rows.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Exact matrices are row-reduced exactly. For machine matrices the rank counts the singular values above `max(r, c)*eps*s_max`, with `s_max` the largest.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
rank([[1, 2], [2, 4]])
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
rank([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
rank([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]])
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
rank([[0, 0], [0, 0]])
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A machine matrix close to singular:

#% cell
rank([[1.0, 2.0], [1.0, 2.0 + 1.0e-20]])
#% output
#= 1

#% text
# A non-square matrix:

#% cell
rank([[1, 0, 1], [0, 1, 1]])
#% output
#= 2

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Rank plus nullity is the number of columns:

#% cell
m = [[1, 2, 3], [2, 4, 6], [1, 1, 1]]; rank(m) + length(null_space(m))
#% output
#= 3

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `null_space`, `row_reduce`, `det`, `singular_values`
